Real competitor context from this same search batch (" + $json.batch_size + " other local businesses in the same category/location search): " + $json.batch_llms_txt_count + " of them have an llms.txt file, and their average mobile PageSpeed score is " + $json.batch_avg_pagespeed + "/100.

TONE FOR THE PITCH: write it the way a friendly local business owner would talk to another business owner they respect — warm, genuine, a little conversational, never clinical or lecture-y. Avoid words like "keyword-stuffed," "excessive," "hurt," "costing you," or anything that sounds like an audit finding. Instead of pointing out what's wrong, frame it as an opportunity you noticed and want to help with. Use the business's own name naturally if you have it. Structure: one genuine, specific observation (can be neutral or even a small compliment) about their site, then one concrete opportunity grounded in a real number or fact, then a low-pressure, curious invitation to chat — not a hard sell. Think "hey, I noticed X, and I bet fixing Y could really help — happy to show you what I mean if you're curious" in spirit, not those exact words. 1-2 short sentences total, sound like an actual person wrote it, not a template.

STRICT RULE: only mention competitors or comparative claims (e.g. "X of the Y businesses we checked...") if the batch numbers above actually support it — e.g. only claim llms.txt adoption among competitors if batch_llms_txt_count is greater than 0. Never assert that competitors are doing something without a real number from the batch context to back it up. If the batch context doesn't support a competitive claim, write the pitch using only this business's own numbers instead.

Respond with ONLY valid JSON, no markdown fences, in this exact shape: {"design_score": <1-10 integer>, "copy_score": <1-10 integer>, "verdict": "<one of: terrible, needs_work, decent, good>", "pitch": "<one sentence, specific, usable as a cold email opener, referencing at least one concrete technical or design issue, and a real competitor stat ONLY if the strict rule above allows it>"}"
  }]
}) }}

